WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.570
Between 2000 and 2020, 677,000
hectares of mangroves were lost,

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.950
before counting new growth.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.930
The largest single driver was aquaculture,

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.630
with 27 per cent.

5
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:20.170
But at Gazi Bay, on Kenya's coast, 1,081
households take part in a project that

6
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.230
earns money by protecting mangroves.

7
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.430
Plan Vivo says the project has
helped provide schoolbooks,

8
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.290
school buildings and clean drinking water.

9
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.170
So who pays to keep the
trees standing, and why?

10
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:33.930
Tourists?

11
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.150
The government?

12
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.630
In this mission, you'll map
where the world's mangroves grow,

13
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.590
and what is clearing them.

14
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.010
Then you'll cross to
the Great Barrier Reef,

15
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.710
and weigh what its managers
can and cannot control.

16
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.490
Your mission: find out who pays
the Gazi Bay community, and why.
